-- title:
--   Proper submodule of an 𝔽ₚ-module of order p² is a line
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $V$ be an additive abelian group carrying a $\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$-module structure whose cardinality (as a natural number, via `Nat.card`) is $p^2$. Let $N$ be a $\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of $V$ with $N \neq \top$, that is, $N$ is a proper submodule, and let $v \in V$ be an element of $N$ with $v \neq 0$. Then $N$ equals the $\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$-span of the singleton $\{v\}$. In other words, a two-dimensional $\mathbb{F}_p$-vector space (presented here through its order $p^2$) has the property that each of its proper submodules containing a nonzero vector $v$ is exactly the line $\mathbb{F}_p v$. Finiteness of $V$ is not assumed separately: it follows from the hypothesis on the cardinality, since $p^2 \neq 0$.
--
--   This is the elementary line-extraction step of linear algebra over $\mathbb{F}_p$: a nonzero proper subspace of a two-dimensional space is the line through any of its nonzero vectors. It is used for the mod-$p$ torsion of an elliptic curve, where $\#E[p] = p^2$, to convert the failure of irreducibility of a mod-$p$ representation into a Galois-stable cyclic subgroup of order $p$ generated by a single torsion point; it is cited in the treatment of the mod $3$ and mod $5$ representations and in the criterion for irreducibility mod $3$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Submodule_eq_span_singleton_of_card_eq_sq.lean

import Mathlib

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
